{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "mq4cs-llamaft-splade", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_10", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "H2 blockers and proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) work differently when it comes to managing acid reflux. H2 blockers, such as cimetidine, ranitidine, and famotidine, work by blocking histamine H2 receptors on stomach cells, thereby reducing acid production. They can decrease acid secretion by up to 70% over a 24-hour period and are useful for managing symptoms like heartburn, especially when taken before meals or at bedtime.  On the other hand, PPIs, like omeprazole, esomeprazole, and lansoprazole, are more potent and provide a long-lasting reduction in acid production. They block the proton pumps in the stomach lining, which are responsible for acid production, achieving comprehensive control over stomach acid. PPIs are typically used for frequent heartburn, which occurs two or more days a week, and are taken daily for optimal effectiveness. They take 1-4 days for full effect, so they are not suitable for instant relief.  If you have kidney concerns, it may be wise to collaborate with your healthcare provider to determine the safest and most effective treatment for your situation.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0016-66-10878:1": 6.130604619670461, "clueweb22-en0034-91-08129:15": 4.687311169461715, "clueweb22-en0030-31-06453:15": 4.578784331178629, "clueweb22-en0041-97-07392:7": 4.497019417412299, "clueweb22-en0012-44-09157:1": 4.052370753433874, "clueweb22-en0045-30-11232:7": 4.050607826836905, "clueweb22-en0021-13-01666:2": 3.9567464512750883, "clueweb22-en0018-84-08858:12": 3.935492667146539, "clueweb22-en0040-31-08349:9": 3.8922108452226114, "clueweb22-en0015-98-04013:10": 3.8661549251869096}, "ptkb_provenance": []}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0016-66-10878:1": 6.130604619670461, "clueweb22-en0034-91-08129:15": 4.687311169461715, "clueweb22-en0030-31-06453:15": 4.578784331178629, "clueweb22-en0041-97-07392:7": 4.4970194